"""Local teaching content and deterministic scoring; no personal data."""
FAQ = [
 ('What is CAN?', 'A broadcast network that lets ECUs exchange compact messages.', 'Classical CAN uses a shared bus and bitwise arbitration. This workbench models standard-ID data messages, not electrical signalling or a complete wire protocol.'),
 ('What is an ECU?', 'An electronic control unit performs a focused vehicle function, such as powertrain control.', 'An ECU combines software, computation and interfaces. One ECU can publish several identifiers; a CAN ID is not an ECU address.'),
 ('Why do vehicles use CAN?', 'It lets controllers share measurements and commands without a dedicated wire for every signal.', 'Priority arbitration and error handling support distributed control. Actual timing depends on bus loading, bitrate, frame format and scheduling.'),
 ('What is a CAN frame?', 'A structured message carrying an identifier and payload, with protocol overhead.', 'Classical data frames carry up to eight payload bytes, plus arbitration, control, CRC and acknowledgement fields. A candump line is a software representation, not every transmitted bit.'),
 ('What is an arbitration ID?', 'A message identifier used for priority and interpretation.', 'Standard CAN uses 11-bit IDs. Extended CAN uses 29-bit IDs. IDs describe message context; they are not generally sender or receiver addresses.'),
 ('How does CAN arbitration work?', 'When nodes start together, dominant bits win over recessive bits without destroying the winning frame.', 'For competing standard data frames, lower numeric IDs have higher priority. Standard/extended and remote-frame details matter in the full protocol. The release timeline sorts simultaneous releases for display. The separate bitwise identifier lab demonstrates dominant/recessive competition without simulating wire timing.'),
 ('What is a DBC file?', 'A specification describing signals inside CAN payloads.', 'It defines IDs, message lengths, signal bit positions, byte order, signedness, scale, offset and units. Community reverse engineering is not automatically OEM verification.'),
 ('How do raw bytes become physical values?', 'Extract a raw integer, then apply physical = raw × scale + offset.', 'For the KUKSA-derived teaching speed, raw 538 × 0.08 − 40 = 3.04 km/h. This validates interpretation according to that definition, not a calibrated speed measurement.'),
 ('What is byte order?', 'It specifies which payload bits contribute the high and low parts of a number.', 'DBC Intel traverses increasing bit numbers from the least significant signal bit. Motorola starts at its most significant bit, counts downward within a byte, then jumps from bit 0 to bit 15 of the next byte. The Frame Laboratory highlights this sawtooth mapping.'),
 ('What is the difference between CAN and CAN FD?', 'CAN FD allows larger payloads and can use a faster data phase.', 'Classical CAN supports up to 8 bytes; CAN FD up to 64 bytes with encoded DLC lengths. FD capability is not implied by a classical parser. These experiments support classical CAN only.'),
 ('What is the difference between CAN and OBD-II?', 'CAN is a communication network; OBD-II defines diagnostic access and emissions-related services.', 'Many OBD-II implementations transport diagnostic services over CAN using higher-level protocols. Ordinary broadcast CAN signals are not automatically standard OBD PIDs.'),
 ('What is message frequency?', 'The number of message arrivals per second.', 'A 100 ms period corresponds to 10 Hz. Logical release period and measured inter-arrival time differ with jitter, capture loss and bus delays.'),
 ('What does a missing message mean?', 'An expected message was not observed within a configured interval.', 'Possible causes include recording gaps, an unplugged logger, changed operating mode, a disabled transmitter or ECU/network issues. Absence alone does not diagnose a failed ECU.'),
 ('What is a virtual ECU?', 'Software that generates or responds to simulated vehicle messages.', 'VCAN reuses its original synthetic signal generator, codec and python-can virtual transport. It does not reproduce an actual Tesla ECU or electrical bus.'),
 ('What is the difference between raw CAN and decoded telemetry?', 'Raw CAN retains IDs and payload bytes; decoded telemetry contains interpreted measurements.', 'Already-decoded workbooks cannot establish the original frame mapping. Sparse Recording observations preserve absent fields rather than inventing a synchronized vehicle state.'),
 ('What is signal provenance?', 'The evidence linking a displayed measurement back to its source.', 'A useful chain includes source hash, frame timestamp/offset, ID/payload, exact DBC, signal bits, scale, offset and unit. Fault experiments reference unchanged input events by index.'),
 ('How does VCAN validate decoded signals?', 'It separates parser, DBC interpretation, plausibility and independent physical validation.', 'The KUKSA investigation demonstrates Level 2 on source-reported real vehicle data. Three manual byte checks pass, but capture attribution and physical accuracy remain unresolved.'),
 ("Why doesn’t DBC decoding prove physical accuracy?", 'A correct formula can still interpret the wrong signal definition or an inaccurate sensor.', 'Independent physical validation needs a compatible capture/DBC plus calibrated or authoritative reference measurements, aligned timestamps and justified tolerances. Another decode of the same DBC is not independent instrumentation.'),
 ('What is anomaly detection?', 'A rule or model that identifies behaviour differing from a defined expectation.', 'These controlled teaching thresholds have known injected ground truth. They are not evaluated v0.5 detectors; production evaluation needs held-out evidence, labels and false-positive measurements.'),
 ('What is the difference between an anomaly and a diagnosed fault?', 'An anomaly is an observation; a fault diagnosis is a supported causal conclusion.', 'A timing deviation could be normal mode change or logger loss. Confirming an ECU defect requires additional evidence and alternative-cause checks.')
]

# Correct choice is indexed explicitly; strings are stable local data, not model output.
QUESTIONS = [
 {'id':'broadcast','level':'Beginner','category':'Fundamentals','question':'Who can observe a CAN broadcast?', 'choices':['All connected receiving nodes','Only the ECU named by the ID','Only an internet server'], 'correct':0,'hint':'Think of a shared bus.','explanation':'CAN broadcasts frames; each receiver decides which IDs matter.'},
 {'id':'payload','level':'Beginner','category':'Frame structure','question':'What is the maximum classical CAN data payload?', 'choices':['64 bytes','8 bytes','1500 bytes'], 'correct':1,'hint':'CAN FD extends this limit.','explanation':'Classical CAN carries at most eight data bytes; CAN FD can carry up to 64.'},
 {'id':'priority','level':'Beginner','category':'Arbitration','question':'Two standard data frames start together: 0x100 and 0x257. Which ID has priority?', 'choices':['0x257','Both always fail','0x100'], 'correct':2,'hint':'Dominant zero bits win.','explanation':'For competing standard data frames the lower numeric identifier has priority. Wire timing is not modelled by the teaching timeline.'},
 {'id':'dbc','level':'Beginner','category':'DBC decoding','question':'What does a DBC primarily provide?', 'choices':['Signal bit positions, scale, offset and units','A guaranteed calibration certificate','A cloud connection'], 'correct':0,'hint':'It describes how to interpret payloads.','explanation':'A DBC specifies interpretation. Compatibility and physical accuracy need additional evidence.'},
 {'id':'scale','level':'Intermediate','category':'Signal scaling','question':'Raw 538, scale 0.08, offset −40 gives what value?', 'choices':['43.04 km/h','3.04 km/h','538 km/h'], 'correct':1,'hint':'Apply the offset after multiplication.','explanation':'538 × 0.08 − 40 = 3.04 km/h, according to the documented teaching definition.'},
 {'id':'timing','level':'Intermediate','category':'Message timing','question':'Expected 100 ms, observed 130 ms, allowed absolute deviation 20 ms. Is the threshold exceeded?', 'choices':['No, 130 is less than 200','Yes, the deviation is 30 ms','This proves an ECU failed'], 'correct':1,'hint':'Compare |observed − expected| to the tolerance.','explanation':'30 ms > 20 ms, so this rule fires. It is timing evidence, not a fault diagnosis.'},
 {'id':'accuracy','level':'Intermediate','category':'Engineering validation','question':'Two tools using the same DBC agree. What does that establish?', 'choices':['Independent sensor calibration','Guaranteed correct vehicle speed','An interpretation cross-check, not independent physical accuracy'], 'correct':2,'hint':'Both tools may share an incorrect source definition.','explanation':'Agreement is useful but a separate measured/authoritative physical reference is required for Level 4.'},
 {'id':'missing','level':'Intermediate','category':'Diagnostic reasoning','question':'A periodic ID disappears from a capture. What is the justified next step?', 'choices':['Inspect timestamps, recording continuity and transmitter conditions','Replace the ECU immediately','Fill missing messages and call them measured'], 'correct':0,'hint':'Consider the logger and operating mode.','explanation':'Missing traffic has several explanations. Preserve evidence and investigate before assigning a cause.'}
]


def score_quiz(level,answers):
    if level not in ('Beginner','Intermediate'):raise ValueError('Unknown quiz level')
    questions=[question for question in QUESTIONS if question['level']==level]
    if not isinstance(answers,dict) or set(answers)-{q['id'] for q in questions}:raise ValueError('Unknown quiz answers')
    feedback=[]
    for question in questions:
        answer=answers.get(question['id'])
        if answer is not None and (type(answer) is not int or not 0<=answer<len(question['choices'])):raise ValueError('Answer index outside choices')
        feedback.append({'id':question['id'],'correct':answer==question['correct'],'answered':answer is not None,'explanation':question['explanation']})
    return {'score':sum(item['correct'] for item in feedback),'total':len(questions),'feedback':feedback}
